# Stable identity for this instance. Autogenerated and persisted if left unset.
# TARGET_INSTANCE_ID=

# --- Slack notifications ---------------------------------------------------
# Who receives them (and the master switch) lives in the UI's Settings view.
# These two only say HOW the hub reaches Slack, and are the preferred way: with
# both set it posts to slack.com/api directly and never touches an MCP.
#
# They are your Slack web session, so grab them with Slack open in a browser:
#   xoxc -> DevTools console:  JSON.parse(localStorage.localConfig_v2).teams
#           then the `token` field of your workspace (starts with "xoxc-").
#   xoxd -> DevTools > Application > Cookies > https://app.slack.com > cookie
#           `d` (starts with "xoxd-"). It is HttpOnly, so the console cannot
#           read it. Paste the value EXACTLY as shown, percent-encoding and all.
#
# Both are needed; either alone is rejected by Slack. Treat them like your
# password: they act as YOU, not as a bot. They also die when you log out of
# that browser (and Slack rotates them), and when they do, the hub logs
# `send-failed: ... invalid_auth` — that means come back here for fresh ones.
#
# Already running a Slack MCP that reads SLACK_MCP_XOXC_TOKEN / SLACK_MCP_XOXD_TOKEN
# (or the bare SLACK_XOXC_TOKEN / SLACK_XOXD_TOKEN)? Those names are accepted too,
# so keep the secret in one place instead of two copies that drift apart.
#
# With these unset the hub falls back to the Slack MCP from `claude /mcp`, and
# with neither available it sends nothing (and says so in the log).
# TARGET_SLACK_XOXC_TOKEN=xoxc-...
# TARGET_SLACK_XOXD_TOKEN=xoxd-...

# --- Hub bind for Docker sandboxes -----------------------------------------
# Workflows with sandbox: docker run steps inside a container. Loopback inside
# the container is not the host, so TCP tools (POST /api/tcps/execute from a
# step) cannot reach a hub bound only to 127.0.0.1 on the machine.
#
# Default: OFF (unset, or any of false/0/off/no). The hub keeps today's bind:
# host 127.0.0.1, port 8893, no automatic sandboxHost.
#
# Set true when you run docker-sandbox workflows that attach TCP tools (or other
# hub API calls from inside the container). On startup the hub applies:
#   host 0.0.0.0, port 8893, sandboxHost 172.17.0.1 (default docker bridge)
# into ~/.target/config.json and restarts listening there. Requires a hub
# restart after changing this file (same as other .env variables).
#
# Exposes the hub on all interfaces — restrict with a firewall or bind manually
# in config.json if you prefer. See README § Containing the agent (docker).
# TARGET_HUB_DOCKER_FRIENDLY=true
